WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.835
-Our first guest tonight is
a talented actress

00:00:02.835 --> 00:00:04.437
you know from "The Newsroom"
and "The Daily Show."

00:00:04.437 --> 00:00:07.774
She stars in "Violet," which is
in select theaters Friday,

00:00:07.774 --> 00:00:09.309
and at home on demand
November 9th.

00:00:09.309 --> 00:00:10.744
Let's take a look.

00:00:10.744 --> 00:00:12.879
-All the benefits of knowing
someone for 20 years, I guess.

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:15.581
-You're a baby.
-Mm-hmm.

00:00:15.581 --> 00:00:17.383
-You know,
I-I feel weird lately.

00:00:17.383 --> 00:00:18.618
-Don't tell her.

00:00:18.618 --> 00:00:19.786
-Are you sick?

00:00:19.786 --> 00:00:23.022
-No, I just -- You know,
the committee in my head.

00:00:23.022 --> 00:00:25.091
You know, I just never really
noticed it much before.

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:26.226
-What committee?

00:00:26.226 --> 00:00:27.426
-You know, the voice that
tells you that

00:00:27.426 --> 00:00:30.563
you're a piece of [bleep]
you know, or whatever.

00:00:30.563 --> 00:00:32.766
I mean, everybody has it.
You know what I'm talking about.

00:00:32.766 --> 00:00:34.267
-I guess.

00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:36.702
-You sound crazy.

00:00:36.702 --> 00:00:38.904
-Please welcome back to the show
Olivia Munn!

00:00:38.904 --> 00:00:41.173
How are you, Olivia?!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:41.173 --> 00:00:43.943
-Hey. How are you, Seth?

00:00:43.943 --> 00:00:46.479
-It's always great to see you.

00:00:46.479 --> 00:00:48.548
And I want to start off
by saying congratulations.

00:00:48.548 --> 00:00:51.884
I know you're expecting a baby.
How's it going?

00:00:51.884 --> 00:00:54.087
-Thanks.
Yeah, it's going really good.

00:00:54.087 --> 00:00:59.726
It's -- it's --
I'm exhausted a lot, I'm tired,

00:00:59.726 --> 00:01:00.960
but it's going really good.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:04.163
I'm getting lots of really
great advice from people

00:01:04.163 --> 00:01:07.466
and lot of confusing advice,
as well,

00:01:07.466 --> 00:01:09.001
but I'm doing really good.

00:01:09.001 --> 00:01:11.771
-Do you find -- I found one
of the most confusing things is,

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:15.041
people would suggest
specific baby gear to you

00:01:15.041 --> 00:01:16.342
that was important to have.

00:01:16.342 --> 00:01:18.344
And the brand names
were impossible

00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:20.747
to keep separate in my head.

00:01:20.747 --> 00:01:22.314
-That's 100% true.

00:01:22.314 --> 00:01:25.051
I mean, there's this
one item called the SNOO

00:01:25.051 --> 00:01:27.086
that people bring up a lot.

00:01:27.086 --> 00:01:29.656
And then, I'm not --
And I'm not quite sure --

00:01:29.656 --> 00:01:31.790
Like, I know it's one
that rocks the baby.

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:34.893
Then there's, like,
one that docks the baby.

00:01:34.893 --> 00:01:37.830
And then there's another
one that, like, props the baby.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.131
And they're all, like --
You're right.

00:01:39.131 --> 00:01:40.733
They're all named
the same thing,

00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:42.668
and I have no idea
what anyone's --

00:01:42.668 --> 00:01:43.836
I mean,
I'm trying to research it.

00:01:43.836 --> 00:01:45.271
I actually have the --
The thing I have

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:47.941
the hardest problem with is,

00:01:47.941 --> 00:01:50.276
I don't know
how many things to get.

00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:51.444
You know, like, I don't know
how many onesies

00:01:51.444 --> 00:01:52.812
you're supposed to get.

00:01:52.812 --> 00:01:54.614
I don't know how many bottles
you're supposed to get,

00:01:54.614 --> 00:01:55.881
how many diapers
you're supposed to have.

00:01:55.881 --> 00:01:58.351
Like, I don't --
People give me a lot of advice,

00:01:58.351 --> 00:02:00.186
but I need real specific --

00:02:00.186 --> 00:02:02.622
-I think you only need
one onesie.

00:02:02.622 --> 00:02:03.856
I think that's why
they're called that.

00:02:03.856 --> 00:02:05.691
[ Laughter ]

00:02:05.691 --> 00:02:07.626
I think it's right
in the name for you.

00:02:07.626 --> 00:02:10.263
Have you received anything
that you would --

00:02:10.263 --> 00:02:12.031
I mean, I know it's impossible
until you actually

00:02:12.031 --> 00:02:13.433
have the baby,
but is there something that

00:02:13.433 --> 00:02:16.436
you feel like
has been good advice?

00:02:16.436 --> 00:02:20.072
-Yeah, there's so much
great advice.

00:02:20.072 --> 00:02:22.575
But I honestly --
The one piece of advice

00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:26.478
that's coming to my head that I
would not classify as great,

00:02:26.478 --> 00:02:27.980
but it's the only one
I'm thinking of right now is,

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:31.417
I have a friend of mine
who said she has a gift

00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:34.787
of being able to tell you
how tall your baby will be.

00:02:34.787 --> 00:02:37.156
And, yeah, so, I said --
She said,

00:02:37.156 --> 00:02:38.791
"Do you want to know
how tall your baby will be?"

00:02:38.791 --> 00:02:42.161
So, I said "Sure."
She said "Okay."

00:02:42.161 --> 00:02:43.329
How tall are you?"

00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:44.330
I said, "I'm 5'5"."

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:45.998
"She goes, "Okay.

00:02:45.998 --> 00:02:48.367
How tall is John?"
I said, "He's 6 feet."

00:02:48.367 --> 00:02:51.437
She goes "Hmm.

00:02:51.437 --> 00:02:54.140
The baby will be 6 feet tall."

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:56.809
Like, I was like --
I don't know where

00:02:56.809 --> 00:02:58.077
she comes up with those figures,

00:02:58.077 --> 00:03:00.880
but, like, that's been, like --

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.550
I get that kind of advice
from people.

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:04.950
-Maybe you need, like --
I don't know --

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:09.656
like, friends with, like, a
firmer background in child-care.

00:03:09.656 --> 00:03:12.191
I do love anybody who's like,
"I'm going to make a prediction"

00:03:12.191 --> 00:03:13.893
where you can't find out
if they're right or wrong

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:15.762
until, like, the kid's 18.

00:03:15.762 --> 00:03:17.363
-I know. I know.

00:03:17.363 --> 00:03:20.366
-"Promise I'm right.
Stay in touch."

00:03:20.366 --> 00:03:22.334
-Unless she's talking about
a 6-foot baby

00:03:22.334 --> 00:03:24.170
that comes out of me.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:26.405
It just stretches.
It just keeps going.

00:03:26.405 --> 00:03:27.606
-I love talking to you
about your mom.

00:03:27.606 --> 00:03:29.175
Is she excited about the fact --
I guess this is

00:03:29.175 --> 00:03:31.143
the second time she's
going to be a grandmother?

00:03:31.143 --> 00:03:35.114
-Yeah, you're right.
She is so excited.

00:03:35.114 --> 00:03:39.718
And she said to me
the other day -- She said,

00:03:39.718 --> 00:03:44.824
"Hey, I want to have the baby
for two weeks, 15 days."

00:03:44.824 --> 00:03:47.493
And I said, "Yeah, you can come
visit whenever you want."

00:03:47.493 --> 00:03:48.994
She goes, "No, no.

00:03:48.994 --> 00:03:53.765
I want to have the baby all by
myself for two weeks, 15 days."

00:03:53.765 --> 00:03:57.437
And I said, "You can come
and have all the time you want

00:03:57.437 --> 00:03:58.805
by yourself with the baby."

00:03:58.805 --> 00:04:00.206
And she said, "No.

00:04:00.206 --> 00:04:03.542
I want to bring the baby
to Oklahoma, at my house,

00:04:03.542 --> 00:04:06.412
all by myself for
two weeks, 15 days."

00:04:06.412 --> 00:04:09.916
And I said, "Why do you want the
baby in Oklahoma, at your house,

00:04:09.916 --> 00:04:12.385
by yourself
for two weeks, 15 days?"

00:04:12.385 --> 00:04:15.788
And she said, "Because I want to
be able to do whatever I want

00:04:15.788 --> 00:04:18.624
with the baby, and the parents
can't tell me no."

00:04:18.624 --> 00:04:23.429
I'm like, "Okay, so,
I'll just give you my baby,

00:04:23.429 --> 00:04:25.164
and you can do
whatever you want."

00:04:25.164 --> 00:04:27.132
So she's not going to get
the baby for two weeks --

00:04:27.132 --> 00:04:32.004
-Yeah. She gave away way too
much of the game in the ask.

00:04:32.004 --> 00:04:34.773
I also like that --
I like that, in her head,

00:04:34.773 --> 00:04:36.242
she thinks she needs two weeks,

00:04:36.242 --> 00:04:38.611
"But just in case
it's not enough,

00:04:38.611 --> 00:04:40.479
I might need that 15th day."

00:04:40.479 --> 00:04:43.082
-Yeah, she needs a travel day
she's added in.

00:04:43.082 --> 00:04:44.083
-You've got to be careful.

00:04:44.083 --> 00:04:46.051
Like, I feel like
when grandparents

00:04:46.051 --> 00:04:48.521
take their grandkids out, like,
they act like

00:04:48.521 --> 00:04:51.590
they have diplomatic immunity
and no rules apply to them.

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:52.758
And, like,
they'll bring back the kids,

00:04:52.758 --> 00:04:53.759
and the kids
will be all amped up.

00:04:53.759 --> 00:04:54.794
And you're like,
"What'd you do?"

00:04:54.794 --> 00:04:56.028
And they're like, "Nothing.

00:04:56.028 --> 00:04:57.363
They had, like,
a coffee and some beef jerky."

00:04:57.363 --> 00:04:59.332
You're like, "No."

00:04:59.332 --> 00:05:03.002
-I remember when my --
we had my niece

00:05:03.002 --> 00:05:04.437
when she was a baby,
and my sister had

00:05:04.437 --> 00:05:06.773
really strict instructions like,
"I don't want her to have --"

00:05:06.773 --> 00:05:09.509
Like, she had,
like, maybe a dairy allergy,

00:05:09.509 --> 00:05:10.509
so she was like,
"I don't want her

00:05:10.509 --> 00:05:12.077
to have this or any sugar yet."

00:05:12.077 --> 00:05:13.779
And then I saw my mom
hand her an ice-cream cone,

00:05:13.779 --> 00:05:14.880
and she goes, "Shh!

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:16.315
Don't tell the mom."

00:05:16.315 --> 00:05:18.985
I was like, "This is crazy.

00:05:18.985 --> 00:05:20.086
Like, you're --
What are you doing?

00:05:20.086 --> 00:05:22.154
Like, the mom
should make the rules."

00:05:22.154 --> 00:05:26.492
And, so, now I remembered that,
so, clearly, I'm like --

00:05:26.492 --> 00:05:29.729
very like --
I'm very wary of her.

00:05:29.729 --> 00:05:31.364
-I think that's
a very good place to be.

00:05:31.364 --> 00:05:33.833
So, this film,
as we tell from the clip --

00:05:33.833 --> 00:05:35.568
or hopefully people
could tell from the clip --

00:05:35.568 --> 00:05:38.237
you play a woman, Violet,
in this film,

00:05:38.237 --> 00:05:40.539
who is competing
with the voice in her head,

00:05:40.539 --> 00:05:42.908
this sort of --
the voice that maybe anxiety

00:05:42.908 --> 00:05:44.176
would put there for us.

00:05:44.176 --> 00:05:48.147
And a pretty incredible actor
playing the voice.

00:05:48.147 --> 00:05:51.284
-Yeah, that's Justin Theroux
playing that insidious voice

00:05:51.284 --> 00:05:55.254
in my head, constantly,
you know, telling me --

00:05:55.254 --> 00:05:58.323
telling Violet how
not good enough she is

00:05:58.323 --> 00:06:00.392
and how, you know,
just everything is going to

00:06:00.392 --> 00:06:02.427
burn to the ground
because of her.

00:06:02.427 --> 00:06:04.864
-Was this part emotionally
exhausting to play,

00:06:04.864 --> 00:06:07.333
to play somebody who's dealing
with these sort of

00:06:07.333 --> 00:06:09.568
internal anxieties?

00:06:09.568 --> 00:06:13.773
-This project was really
emotionally exhausting for me.

00:06:13.773 --> 00:06:16.575
I had actually gone from
two independent movies

00:06:16.575 --> 00:06:18.176
right into this
third independent movie

00:06:18.176 --> 00:06:19.345
back-to-back-to-back.

00:06:19.345 --> 00:06:22.647
So I was already so
emotionally exhausted,

00:06:22.647 --> 00:06:24.450
physically exhausted.

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:26.352
And, I mean,
I am one of those people

00:06:26.352 --> 00:06:28.454
that struggles with
that internal dialogue.

00:06:28.454 --> 00:06:32.191
That's that voice that's always
telling me, you know,

00:06:32.191 --> 00:06:33.826
so many things that just create

00:06:33.826 --> 00:06:35.128
so much self-doubt
and self-hate.

00:06:35.128 --> 00:06:37.029
I mean,
I struggle with that a lot.

00:06:37.029 --> 00:06:40.399
And I've worked really hard to
put that voice into a box,

00:06:40.399 --> 00:06:42.668
and so then,
to go on to this film

00:06:42.668 --> 00:06:46.205
and be doing this role
with this voice --

00:06:46.205 --> 00:06:47.573
And then there's
Justine Bateman,

00:06:47.573 --> 00:06:50.943
who directed the film, and
she's yelling on the microphone,

00:06:50.943 --> 00:06:54.413
you know, like, just screaming
out all these things to you.

00:06:54.413 --> 00:06:56.048
It was really
emotionally draining

00:06:56.048 --> 00:06:57.783
and it was so exhausting,
and there was times

00:06:57.783 --> 00:07:00.619
that I just broke down crying
in the middle of a scene,

00:07:00.619 --> 00:07:02.187
in the scenes where you're not
supposed to be crying,

00:07:02.187 --> 00:07:03.756
but it's okay if I cried.

00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:05.858
And I'm crying,
I'm breaking down crying,

00:07:05.858 --> 00:07:08.928
and then they yell "cut,"
and I'm still crying.

00:07:08.928 --> 00:07:11.530
And I'm just kind of trying to
remove myself to collect myself,

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:13.065
and then the A.D. comes over.

00:07:13.065 --> 00:07:14.867
And they're like -- 'Cause it's
an independent movie,

00:07:14.867 --> 00:07:17.469
so time is of the essence,
and there's no money.

00:07:17.469 --> 00:07:21.607
He's like, "Hey, um, really need
you to, like, stop crying

00:07:21.607 --> 00:07:24.644
and just get back to the scene."

00:07:24.644 --> 00:07:26.445
And I was like, "Oh, okay."

00:07:26.445 --> 00:07:28.848
Just trying to, like, reckon --

00:07:28.848 --> 00:07:30.682
It was just like,
just bury it deep down,

00:07:30.682 --> 00:07:31.951
like my mom taught me.

00:07:31.951 --> 00:07:34.987
Just bury all that stuff and
just keep on going.

00:07:34.987 --> 00:07:36.456
-See? That's what
she can teach your baby

00:07:36.456 --> 00:07:39.258
when she has her
for two weeks, 15 days.

00:07:39.258 --> 00:07:40.459
Just bury your emotions.

00:07:40.459 --> 00:07:42.195
Hey, thanks so much
for being here, Olivia.

00:07:42.195 --> 00:07:44.997
Congratulations on the baby.
Congratulations on the movie.

00:07:44.997 --> 00:07:46.365
[ Cheers and applause ]
We'll see you soon.

00:07:46.365 --> 00:07:49.035
-Thank you.
-Olivia Munn, everybody.

00:07:49.035 --> 00:07:51.671
"Violet" is in
select theaters Friday

00:07:51.671 --> 00:07:52.905
and at home on demand
November 9th.

